    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Let $t = x/y>1$.  Then $x = ty$, and $x^2 + y^2 = t^2 y^2 + y^2 = y^2 (t^2 + 1)$.  Hence\n\\[\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2+ y^2} = \\frac{t^2 y y + y^2}{y^2 (t^2 + 1)} = \\frac{(t^2 + 1) y + t^2 y}{(t^2 + 1)y} = 1 + \\frac{t^2}{t^2 + 1}\\]Since $t>1$, $t^2 + 1 > t^2$, and $\\frac{1}{t^2 + 1} < \\frac{1}{t^2}$.  Therefore\n\\[\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} = 1 + \\frac{t^2}{t^2 + 1} < 1 + \\frac{t^2}{1} = 2.\\]For example, $x = \\sqrt{t}$ and $y = 1$ gives a value of $\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} = 2$ for $t > 1$.  Thus\n\\[\\boxed{2}\\]is the maximum possible value.\nWhat is the maximum value of \\[(x^{2k} + y^{2k})/(x^{2k - 1} + y^{2k - 1})\\]for positive real numbers, $x$ and $y$, and integer $k\\geq 2$?",
